Fixed by #282, merged as c1b506d.

`agentRules: false` in `apps/web/next.config.js`. The write is gated on that key in `node_modules/next/dist/server/lib/app-info-log.js`:

```js
async function ensureAgentRulesForDev(dir) {
    if (await getAgentName() === null) return null;
    if (hasCurrentAgentRules(dir)) return null;
    return writeAgentFiles(dir);
}
```

and `start-server.js` calls it only when `agentRules !== false`. Verified by running `next dev` on a clean tree with the key set: `apps/web/AGENTS.md` is untouched.

## Full disclosure on how this got fixed

I hit this independently while working #228 — `next dev` rewrote `AGENTS.md` underneath a guard that reads `next.config.js` — and filed it as part of #275 without first checking whether it was already open. It was, here, with better analysis than mine. #282 fixed it as a coupled part of that change rather than as a fix for this issue, so it merged without referencing this number.

Two details found separately that are worth adding to the record here:

**The write is narrower than "every `next dev`".** `getAgentName()` returns non-null only under a detected coding agent — `CLAUDECODE`, `GEMINI_CLI`, `CURSOR_AGENT`, `CURSOR_TRACE_ID`, `AI_AGENT`, `CODEX_*`, `COPILOT_*`, `AUGMENT_AGENT`, `OPENCODE_CLIENT`, `ANTIGRAVITY_AGENT`, `REPL_ID`, or `/opt/.devin` existing (`node_modules/next/dist/compiled/@vercel/detect-agent/index.js`). A developer running `make dev-web` from a plain shell never saw it. That explains how it survived: it fires for exactly the population least likely to question an `AGENTS.md` diff, and not for anyone else.

**`apps/web/CLAUDE.md` was never at risk**, which I had assumed it was until I measured it. `generate-agent-files.js` takes this branch:

```js
if (agentsMdExists && (agentsMdHostsBlock || !claudeMdHostsBlock)) {
    return { agentsMd: upsertFile(agentsMdPath, block), claudeMd: 'skipped' }
}
```

`AGENTS.md` exists and `CLAUDE.md` carries no marker, so `CLAUDE.md` is explicitly skipped. The pointer-file discipline and `make agent-docs-check` were never going to catch this, exactly as this issue says — but not because the check is weak; because the write deliberately avoids the file the check guards.
